      <workList xml:id="workDesc_27161673">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e174171">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_28fd324f">B2:434</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_160N2008E" xml:lang="de">Singgedicht auf den Geburtstag des Königs von Preussen</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_6b8d4e77">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_160N20099">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_160N200A1">Johann Andreas Cramer</persName>
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_160N2009D">Friedrich II (Friedrich der Große)</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_160N200A4">
               <p xml:id="idm75">The only information on the present cantata, composed for the birthday of <persName xml:id="idm76" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Frederick the Great</persName>, is a newspaper advertisement that appeared in 1780. The work, which was a complete score, was part of a lot including musical works by late eighteenth-century composers such as <persName xml:id="idm77" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Benda</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm78" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Zielche</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm79" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Graun</persName>, and <persName xml:id="idm80" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Pugnani</persName> as well as six works by Scheibe. There is no indication in the advertisement as to whom the seller was. The text author of the present cantata was the royal Chaplain, <persName xml:id="idm81" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Andreas Cramer</persName>. During the early years of the Seven Years’ War (1756–63) when Scheibe was living in Sønderborg, he seems to have sought a closer connection to patrons at the court of Prussia, and it is possible that the work was composed sometime in the late 1750s. On one of his many trips to Copenhagen Scheibe must have met Cramer who arrived in the city in 1754.</p>
            </history>
